# Worked solutions: 5.68 - 5.102 (frequency response, part 3). 5.64 and 5.100 are left out on purpose: see CONFLICTS in the summary.
S = {
"5.68": ("B", "Phase crossover: the denominator of $G(j\\omega)$ must be purely real.", "$G(j\\omega)=\\dfrac{K}{(6-6\\omega^2)+j(11\\omega-\\omega^3)}$.\nThe phase is $-180^\\circ$ when the imaginary part of the denominator is zero: $11\\omega-\\omega^3=0\\Rightarrow\\omega=\\sqrt{11}$ rad/s (frequency is positive): **(b)**."),
"5.69": ("D", "Bode plot of a differentiator.", "$G(j\\omega)=j\\omega$: magnitude $20\\log\\omega$, rising at $20$ dB/decade (2); phase constant at $+90^\\circ=\\pi/2$ (4): **(d)**."),
"5.70": ("C", "Properties of Bode plots.", "1. GM and PM are read directly from the plots: true.\n2. The Bode (and Nyquist-from-Bode) stability tests are valid for minimum-phase systems: true.\n3. A positive gain margin needs the magnitude at phase crossover to be *below* 0 dB, not positive: false.\n\nOnly 1 and 2: **(c)**."),
"5.71": ("A", "Characteristic equation.", "The roots of $1+G(s)H(s)=0$ are, by definition, the poles of the closed-loop transfer function $\\dfrac{G}{1+GH}$: **(a)**."),
"5.72": ("C", "Right-half-plane zero.", "$G(s)=\\dfrac{10(s-1)}{s+10}$ has a zero at $s=+1$ in the right half-plane, while its pole ($-10$) is stable: a stable non-minimum-phase system: **(c)**."),
"5.73": ("B", "$M_r$ and $\\omega_r$ of the closed loop.", "Closed loop: $\\dfrac{25}{s^2+6s+25}$, so $\\omega_n=5$ and $\\xi=0.6$.\n$M_r=\\dfrac1{2\\xi\\sqrt{1-\\xi^2}}=\\dfrac1{2(0.6)(0.8)}=1.04$.\n$\\omega_r=\\omega_n\\sqrt{1-2\\xi^2}=5\\sqrt{0.28}=2.65\\approx2.67$ rad/s: **(b)**."),
"5.74": ("C", "Corner frequency $=\\omega_n$ of each quadratic factor.", "Numerator $s^2+10s+100$: $\\omega_n=10$ rad/s.\nDenominator $s^2+15s+1$: $\\omega_n=1$ rad/s (the double pole at the origin has no corner).\n\n$10$ and $1$ rad/s: **(c)**."),
"5.75": ("B", "Asymptotic plot of a PD controller.", "$0.1+0.01s=0.1(1+0.1s)$: DC level $-20$ dB, corner at $10$ rad/s, then $+20$ dB/decade.\nTo rise from $-20$ dB to $+20$ dB takes two decades above the corner: $10\\times100=1000$ rad/s: **(b)**."),
"5.76": ("A", "Phase margin condition at gain crossover.", "$PM=180^\\circ-90^\\circ-\\tan^{-1}\\dfrac{\\omega_{gc}}5=45^\\circ\\Rightarrow\\omega_{gc}=5$ rad/s.\n$|G|=\\dfrac{K}{5\\sqrt{25+25}}=1\\Rightarrow K=35.4\\approx35$: **(a)**."),
"5.77": ("D", "Margins of an unstable system.", "Both margins are usually negative (3). But with several crossover frequencies (conditionally stable or non-minimum-phase loops) one margin can be positive while the other is negative, in either order (1, 2). All three can occur: **(d)**."),
"5.78": ("D", "Phase of a lead-lag ratio.", "$\\angle G=\\tan^{-1}0.2\\omega-\\tan^{-1}0.5\\omega$.\nAt $\\omega=0$: $0^\\circ$. At $\\omega=\\infty$: $90^\\circ-90^\\circ=0^\\circ$: **(d)**."),
"5.79": ("B", "Slope changes show corner frequencies.", "The initial slope of $-40$ dB/decade means two poles at the origin. At $0.1$ the slope rises by $+20$ (a zero) and at $10$ it rises by another $+20$ (a second zero).\n\nTwo poles and two zeros: **(b)**."),
"5.80": ("B", "All-pass systems.", "An all-pass system has unit magnitude at all frequencies; its poles and zeros are mirror images about the $j\\omega$ axis (a symmetric pole-zero pattern), e.g. $\\dfrac{s-a}{s+a}$: **(b)**."),
"5.81": ("C", "Relative stability.", "Relative stability is expressed through the gain margin, phase margin and the location of the poles in the $s$-plane (3), and it is also a comparison with another system (4). It is not defined by gain margin alone or phase margin alone.\n\n3 and 4: **(c)**."),
"5.82": ("C", "Read the asymptotic magnitude plot.", "The plot is flat at $20$ dB up to $1$ rad/s, then falls at $-20$ dB/decade.\n$20$ dB $=10$ and a corner at $1$ rad/s give $G=\\dfrac{10}{s+1}$: **(c)**."),
"5.83": ("A", "Non-minimum-phase elements.", "(I) True: non-minimum-phase elements add extra phase lag that grows with frequency, which complicates compensation.\n(II) True: a transportation lag $e^{-sT}$ is non-minimum phase, and its phase lag $-\\omega T$ grows without limit, which is the cause of (I).\n\n**(a)**."),
"5.84": ("C", "Low-frequency slope $=-20\\times$ (number of poles at the origin).", "Two poles at the origin give a low-frequency slope of $-40$ dB/decade: **(c)**."),
"5.85": ("B", "Read the slope changes.", "Slope $-20$ at the start means one pole at the origin. At $2$ rad/s the slope steepens to $-40$ (a pole at $2$). At $20$ rad/s it returns to $-20$ (a zero at $20$).\n\n$G(s)H(s)=\\dfrac{K(s+20)}{s(s+2)}$: **(b)**."),
"5.86": ("C", "Slope changes show the number of poles.", "The slope is $+40$ dB/decade at the start, so $s^2$ in the numerator. At $10$ rad/s the slope drops by $100$ dB/decade (to $-60$), which is five poles at $10$.\n\n$\\dfrac{Ks^2}{(1+s/10)^5}$: **(c)**."),
"5.87": (None, "Nyquist: $N=Z-P$.", "For closed-loop stability $Z=0$, so the required encirclement of $-1+j0$ is $N=-P$ (counter-clockwise). The count of right-half-plane poles $P$ depends on how the poles \"on the imaginary axis\" are treated in the description. The question is ambiguous, so no answer key has been set."),
"5.88": ("A", "Transport lag in Bode plots.", "(II) True: $e^{-sT}$ has magnitude 1 and phase $-\\omega T$, so only the phase plot shifts.\n(I) True: that is exactly why a Bode plot handles delays easily (add $-\\omega T$ to the phase). (II) is the reason for (I).\n\n**(a)**."),
"5.89": ("D", "High-pass transfer functions.", "A high-pass response needs a zero at the origin and equal numbers of poles and zeros at high frequency (so the gain stays constant), e.g. $\\dfrac{s}{s+a}$ and $\\dfrac{s^2}{s^2+bs+c}$. A function with 2 poles and 1 zero rolls off at high frequency, so it is band-pass.\n\n1 and 2 only: **(d)**."),
"5.90": ("D", "Definition of frequency response.", "The magnitude and phase relationship between a sinusoidal input and the steady-state output is the frequency response: **(d)**."),
"5.91": ("A", "Minimum-phase functions.", "All poles and zeros in the left half-plane define a minimum-phase transfer function: **(a)**."),
"5.92": ("B", "Resonant frequency.", "The frequency at which the magnitude peaks is the resonant frequency $\\omega_r$: **(b)**."),
"5.93": ("D", "Margins of stable and unstable systems.", "(I) False: an unstable system has a negative gain margin or a *negative* phase margin, not a positive one.\n(II) True: a stable system needs both margins positive.\n\n**(d)**."),
"5.94": ("B", "$M$ and $N$ circles.", "Constant-$M$ circles give constant closed-loop *magnitude*; constant-$N$ circles give constant closed-loop *phase* of a unity-feedback system: **(b)**."),
"5.95": ("A", "Asymptotic phase at 20 Hz.", "Each pole or zero contributes about $\\mp90^\\circ$ if the frequency is well above its corner and about $0^\\circ$ if well below.\nPoles at $0.01$ Hz and $1$ Hz: $-90^\\circ-90^\\circ$. Pole at $80$ Hz: $\\approx0^\\circ$. Zero at $5$ Hz: $+90^\\circ$. Zeros at $100$ and $200$ Hz: $\\approx0^\\circ$.\n\nTotal $\\approx-90^\\circ$: **(a)**."),
"5.96": (None, "Second-order peak: $M_r=\\dfrac{1}{2\\xi\\sqrt{1-\\xi^2}}$, $\\omega_r=\\omega_n\\sqrt{1-2\\xi^2}$.", "DC gain 5, peak $\\dfrac{10}{\\sqrt3}$ so $M_r=\\dfrac{2}{\\sqrt3}$, which gives $\\xi=0.5$. $\\omega_r=5\\sqrt2=\\omega_n\\sqrt{0.5}\\Rightarrow\\omega_n=10$.\nThe system is $\\dfrac{500}{s^2+10s+100}$, which is not among the options (option (c) has $12s$ instead of $10s$). The question as transcribed has an error, so no answer key has been set."),
"5.97": ("C", "Resonant peak.", "$M_r=\\dfrac{1}{2\\zeta\\sqrt{1-\\zeta^2}}$ for $0<\\zeta<0.707$: **(c)**."),
"5.98": ("C", "Phase margin at gain crossover.", "$|G(j\\omega)|=\\dfrac{2\\sqrt3}{\\omega\\sqrt{1+\\omega^2}}=1\\Rightarrow\\omega^2(1+\\omega^2)=12\\Rightarrow\\omega=\\sqrt3$.\n$\\angle G=-90^\\circ-\\tan^{-1}\\sqrt3=-150^\\circ$, so $PM=30^\\circ$: **(c)**."),
"5.99": ("D", "No gain crossover.", "$|G(j\\omega)|^2=\\dfrac{1+\\omega^2}{(1+\\omega^2)(4+\\omega^2)}=\\dfrac1{4+\\omega^2}\\le0.25$ for all $\\omega$. The magnitude never reaches 1, so there is no gain crossover frequency and the phase margin is unlimited ($\\infty$): **(d)**."),
"5.101": (None, "Bode plot of a type-2 system.", "$|G|=K/\\omega^2$ at low frequency, so the $-40$ dB/decade line crosses 0 dB at $\\omega_x=\\sqrt K$ (not $K$), has the value $20\\log K$ at $\\omega=1$ (not $40\\log K$), and $K$ is the *acceleration* error coefficient (not the ramp one).\nAs transcribed all three statements are wrong, which is not among the options, so no answer key has been set."),
"5.102": ("A", "Phase and gain crossover definitions.", "1. Gain margin measures how close the negative-real-axis crossing is to the critical point: correct.\n2. The phase crossover point is where the plot cuts the *negative* real axis, not the positive one: **not correct**.\n3. The phase crossover frequency is where $\\angle G(j\\omega)H(j\\omega)=-180^\\circ$ ($\\equiv180^\\circ$): correct.\n\nOnly statement 2 is not correct: **(a)**."),
}
